# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# Fourth (final) compression pass for the 30-page version.
S = lambda n: "Section §§" + n + "§§"

T3 = ("The second is the department's willingness to pursue its doctrine independently of the elected government. In "
      "1976 the Treasury negotiated the largest loan in the IMF's history, while the Labour Cabinet was split, on "
      "austerity terms justified by debt figures later shown to have been grossly overstated in the documents submitted "
      "to the Fund; Edmund Dell, then Paymaster General, defended the exaggeration in print as necessary to secure cuts "
      "the department believed essential (Dell, 1991; López, 2014). The Department of Economic Affairs, created in "
      "1964 to divide the Treasury's authority, was re-absorbed within five years (Clifford, 1997). These are existence "
      "proofs; a milder disposition is all the mechanism requires. The Office for Budget Responsibility, created in 2010 "
      "with a staff of fewer than twenty under a Charter the Treasury writes (Berry and Berry, 2013; Walsh, 2021), "
      "inherited machinery linking demography to fiscal sustainability that had existed within the Treasury and the "
      "Government Actuary's Department throughout the sample; its migration variants (OBR, 2014) publish arithmetic the "
      "fiscal centre had long performed internally.")
CM2 = ("Under the work-permit system, in force until 2008, a non-EU national required a job offer from an employer who "
       "had shown under the Resident Labour Market Test that the vacancy could not be filled domestically, and a visa "
       "could be refused even after a permit was granted; by the mid-2000s more than eighty routes existed (Home Office, "
       "2006, p. 7), and the Rules have since grown to some 375,000 words (Guardian, 2018). Absent published quotas, the "
       "dual approval gave the state continuous discretionary control over volumes, and it dictates the measurement "
       "strategy: a Statement of Changes may loosen some routes while tightening others, permit data are too fragmentary "
       "for statistical use (Bauer and Salt, 2020), and no stringency series spans the period, so policy is measured by "
       "its outcomes on the controlled margin. That the lever moved with demography, quietly, is visible administratively: "
       "in 1990 the Overseas Labour Section recorded that “because of demographic changes the number of work permits "
       "being granted is rising sharply” while asking that “actual figures” be “omitted as they can be "
       "misleading” (The National Archives, LAB 109/66).")
DR3 = ("For the turn-of-the-millennium expansion the mechanism is documented directly, in drafts released under the "
       "Freedom of Information Act (Cabinet Office, 2010) of the government's first comprehensive economic analysis of "
       "migration, prepared by the Cabinet Office Performance and Innovation Unit and the Home Office and published in "
       "January 2001 (Glover et al., 2001). The July 2000 draft, classified RESTRICTED–POLICY, reasons in the terms "
       "of Section 3: without net migration the working-age population would fall by two million over twenty-five years "
       "while the over-65 population rose by three million; ageing “will have to be addressed: presumably by some "
       "combination of net migration, an increase in the average working life, changes to entitlements, increases in "
       "contributions (either tax-based or private), and increases in productivity,” since “the alternative to "
       "migration may often be tax increases”; and these pressures would recast immigrants “less like a threat "
       "and more as a way to avoid the more unpleasant alternatives of tax increases, benefit cuts, and economic "
       "stagnation.” None of this survived to publication: the quantified requirements, the instrument menu, the "
       "comparison with taxation, and the “unpleasant alternatives” passage are all absent by the November "
       "draft, the autumn in which ministers announced “managed migration”. The episode documents the reasoning "
       "and its removal; it covers one episode under one government, in the Cabinet Office's and Home Office's voice "
       "rather than the Treasury's (" + S("7") + ").")
CT1 = ("The institutional literature specifies the Treasury's inputs as the fiscal aggregates and the labour market, "
       "observed with a lag (Heclo and Wildavsky, 1974); the controls are the previous year's values of three series. "
       "*Tax per working-age person*, in thousands of constant 2024–25 pounds, is national accounts taxes (OBR, 2026; HM Treasury, 2026) "
       "divided by the working-age population; the per-taxpayer basis rather than tax-to-GDP is the appropriate measure "
       "because the political cost attaches to the burden per taxpayer, and the variable measures how far the intensive "
       "margin has already been used. *The annual log change in net debt as a share of GDP* measures the rate at which "
       "the borrowing margin is closing. *The ILO unemployment rate* (ONS, 2026) is the Budget-time reading of the labour "
       "market. Candidates tested and excluded, the inactivity rate, growth and productivity, gilt yields (Federal "
       "Reserve Bank of St. Louis, 2025), and the Budget-time growth forecast digitised via the Economic Statistics "
       "Centre of Excellence (2026), are documented in Section 6.1.")
PJ5 = ("Assembling the series required reconstructing the full history of vintages (Online Appendix A). The ONS "
       "publishes complete projections only back to 2014, and earlier vintages exist as scans on an unindexed National "
       "Archives site navigable only to 1998; a machine-readable annex to the 2008-based NPP was found to link every "
       "predecessor back to 1954 (ONS, 2016b), and through it the projected OADR at each horizon was extracted for every "
       "vintage from the 1977-based to the 2018-based, the most recent vintage carried forward between releases. "
       "Multi-vintage records had been assembled only from within the statistical service (Shaw, 2007); this is, to my "
       "knowledge, the first horizon-complete panel from public sources and the first use of the vintages as an "
       "information-set regressor. Vintages are assigned by base year, an assumption confronted in Section 5.2, and the "
       "legislated female pension-age rise is embedded in the relevant vintages.")
R1 = ("A brief account of the path to the final specification guards against the impression of a curated result. The "
      "starting point was a five-year projection window, matching the electoral cycle and the Treasury's review "
      "horizons, in a level-form regression on the tax take, unemployment, inactivity, and productivity growth; it "
      "produced a strongly significant projection coefficient but failed the Breusch–Godfrey test (p = 0.018), "
      "motivating the autoregressive term, and an interaction between projection and tax burden generated extreme "
      "collinearity (maximum VIF above 100). No measure of growth attained significance; neither gilt yield mattered, "
      "suggesting the debt stock rather than its price is the constraint; the Budget-time growth forecast destabilised "
      "the other coefficients; and the near-time-invariant inactivity rate was dropped when degrees of freedom required. "
      "The level-form ARDL passed all diagnostics except RESET, which favoured logs; in logs the best-fitting horizon "
      "lengthens from five to ten years, matching the statutory minimum horizon for the government's own long-term "
      "projections (" + S("4.2") + "): the specification the data select is the one the fiscal framework mandates.")
PE = ("The structural-change literature attributes the expansion to a liberal policy era after 1997 (Mitchell, Pain and "
      "Riley, 2011), and the horse race can be run directly. Their indicator, POL97, is a step from 1998 onwards, not a "
      "window closing in 2007, a distinction invisible in a sample ending in 2007 but real here: the non-EU inflow rate "
      "averages 2.8 per thousand before 1998, 6.5 during the window and 6.7 after it. Tested faithfully, the step carries "
      "more weight than the window, 0.14 (p = 0.07), or 0.17 lagged as they enter it (p = 0.05), against 0.012 (p = 0.84) "
      "for the window, and the projection survives either way, at 0.0059 and 0.0071. Their full dummy set does no "
      "better: POL97, POL02 for the 2002 skilled-worker route, and the enlargement indicators for 1986, 1995 and 2004 "
      "are jointly irrelevant (F(5, 30) = 1.67, p = 0.17) while the projection holds at 0.0070 (p = 0.011). The "
      "collinearity is the point: the ten-year delta averages 0.9 points before 1998, 14.9 within the window, and "
      "−4.5 after 2007, so the indicator nearly discretises the projection; interacted with the projection, the step "
      "enters negatively (−0.0068, p = 0.37), the opposite of what an account resting on the post-1997 government "
      "requires. In Mitchell, Pain and Riley's own transformation, the annual change in the log migration rate, which "
      "renders both sides stationary at the cost of the persistence carrying most of the variation (adjusted R² of "
      "0.07), the projection is the only variable that registers: 0.0062 (p = 0.099) for non-EU and 0.0072 (p = 0.033) "
      "for total inflows, while the lagged POL97, POL02 and the 2004 enlargement return p = 0.68, 0.79 and 0.71. "
      "Conditional on the government's own ageing projections, the policy era has no explanatory power in their "
      "specification or in mine.")
FR = ("The remaining checks are reported in Online Appendix B. The result does not depend on the per-capita scaling: on "
      "gross non-EU inflows the coefficient is 0.0080 (HAC s.e. 0.0014) in the fiscal and 0.0073 (0.0015) in the trend "
      "specification, and in levels each further projected point is associated with roughly 1,300 to 1,400 additional "
      "arrivals a year. On the LTIM series from 1991 it remains significant at five per cent (0.0103, s.e. 0.0038, N = "
      "28). The projected level in place of the change gives 0.0080; the term is significant at three, five, seven, and "
      "ten years, with ten fitting best, and the one-year change alone carries no signal (p = 0.56). Starting the sample "
      "in 1983 or 1987 returns 0.0069 and 0.0062, both significant at five per cent. What the battery cannot exclude is "
      "a confounder sharing the projection's exact profile, fiscal, pensioner-specific, operative only on the controlled "
      "margin, and timed to the vintages; none has suggested itself, and closing that residual vulnerability is what "
      "the agenda of " + S("7") + " is built for.")
X3 = ("*Limitations and agenda.* Three limitations are material: the zero-migration reconstruction treats all net "
      "migrants as working-age; the dependent variable pools work, study, and family entry, so the implication that the "
      "response concentrates where discretion and youth are greatest is testable only with administrative data; and "
      "outside the 2000–2001 episode the evidence for the mechanism is institutional and statistical. Each admits a "
      "remedy. The biennial NPP releases can be exploited as information shocks, the revision between vintages "
      "decomposed into mortality, fertility, and migration-assumption components supplying news orthogonal to migration "
      "by construction, with Home Office quarterly visa grants by category as an outcome of sufficient frequency for an "
      "event-study design around release dates. The mechanism can be taken to the Treasury files already inside the "
      "thirty-year declassification window, to undigitised Treasury forecast material (HM Treasury, 1997), and to text "
      "analysis of Budget documents. External validity can be tested in a cross-country panel on UN projection vintages, "
      "and the pension-age and migration instruments modelled jointly.")
